/K [1]
>>
endobj
471 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (If we  see the demographic structure of Bangladesh we can see that we are experiencing rapid population ageing, with the number of elderly people \(60+\) growing much faster than the overall population. The elderly population was 9.28% of the total population in 2022, up from 7.48% in 2011, and is projected to reach about 22% by 2050. Which means that The demand for the medicine will grow with the rising aging population. )
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [12 13]
>>
endobj
472 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Also Along female population is increasing as well In 2024, females made up approximately 50.8% of Bangladesh's total population and females are also affected by numerous of diseases like  Polycystic Ovary Syndrome \(PCOS\)  and many more. )
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
473 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (In our country aging is associated with a significant increase in the prevalence and burden of diseases. particularly chronic conditions or multimorbidity which refers to the co-occurrence of two or more chronic diseases in an individual. Studies have highlighted the prevalence of various health issues among older adults in Bangladesh and the factors associated with their development.  A 2025 study among adults aged 50 and over found the prevalence of multimorbidity in Bangladesh was around 39% \(Mohsin et al., 2025\) . )
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [6 7 8]
>>
endobj
474 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 223 0 R
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
475 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Overall, in our county households' out-of-pocket spending on medicines is high, with 87.9% of households incurring such costs, though the poorest households allocate a larger share of their total spending to medicines \(Rakin-Uz-Zaman, 2022\) . )
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [1 2]
>>
endobj
476 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (The overall picture clearly indicates that the demand for medicines in Bangladesh will continue to grow in the future. With rapid urbanisation, lifestyle changes, and the steady rise of chronic diseases such as diabetes, hypertension, and cardiovascular complications, healthcare expenditure per household is already significant and projected to increase further. This shows the immense financial weight of long-term treatment.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
477 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (At the same time, demographic shifts are amplifying this trend. The ageing population is expanding rapidly meaning that age related illnesses will drive sustained medicine demand. Moreover, with females making up over half \(50.8%\) of the population, gender-specific health concerns such as PCOS and other chronic conditions will add to pharmaceutical needs.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
478 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Taken together, these factors rising disease burden increasing life expectancy, a growing middle class, and gender-specific health demands all point to a strong and expanding domestic pharmaceutical market. Thus, the pharma sector in Bangladesh holds significant potential for future growth, both in terms of meeting local healthcare needs and strengthening its position as a vital industry in the national economy)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
